      "\u2728 What if I told you that preparing for pregnancy isn\u2019t just a woman\u2019s responsibility?",
    summary: "Patient education from @womenshealthduo on Instagram.",
    instagramCaption:
      "\u2728 What if I told you that preparing for pregnancy isn\u2019t just a woman\u2019s responsibility?\n\nWe\u2019ve normalized telling women to start folic acid, eat healthier, exercise, and avoid harmful habits before trying to conceive.\n\nBut how often do we tell future dads to prepare too?\n\nThe truth is, conception is a team effort.\n\nEmerging research shows that a father\u2019s health before conception may influence sperm quality, early embryo development, placental function, and even aspects of a child\u2019s long-term health through epigenetic changes. This doesn\u2019t change the baby\u2019s DNA, but it may influence how certain genes are expressed.\n\nThe encouraging part? Sperm is constantly being produced and takes around 70\u201390 days to mature. That means the habits a man builds in the 3 months before conception can influence the quality of the sperm involved in conception.\n\nThis isn\u2019t about placing pressure or blame on fathers.\n\nIt\u2019s about recognizing that both parents have an opportunity to support a healthier pregnancy before it even begins. Preconception care should be a conversation for couples.. not just women.\n\n\u{1F447}\u{1F3FD} Did this surprise you? Tell us in the comments:\nBefore today, did you know a father\u2019s health could influence pregnancy even before conception?\n\n\u{1F4AC} Tag your partner or someone planning a pregnancy.\n\n\u{1F4CC} Save this post so you remember to prepare together, not just individually.\n\n\u{1F4DA} Evidence matters.\nSystematic reviews and recent studies suggest that paternal obesity, smoking, poor nutrition, alcohol use and chronic stress can alter sperm quality and epigenetic marks. These changes have been associated with differences in embryo development, placental function and offspring health, although more human studies are still needed to understand the full extent of these effects.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n.\n#preconception #pregnancy #spermhealth #womenshealth #futuredad",
