    doctor: "charmi",
    topics: ["Fertility", "Menstrual health", "Carousel"],
    title:
      "Ideally, you should begin seeing your gynaecologist in your early adolescence so that you can develop a trusting rela\u2026",
    summary: "Patient education from @womenshealthduo on Instagram.",
    instagramCaption:
      'Ideally, you should begin seeing your gynaecologist in your early adolescence so that you can develop a trusting relationship with your doctor in the long run. Certain changes in your body may become apparent as you age. Here are some warning signs to look out for:\n\n1) DYSMENORRHEA - This is one of the most common symptoms experienced by women. It is described as severe cramping. It is usually the result of prostaglandin release. Prostaglandin causes the muscles of the uterus to contract, causing discomfort and pain. The level of intensity varies from person to person.\n\n2) IRREGULAR MENSES - This usually occurs in adolescents who are just starting their period as their bodies adjust to hormonal changes. A normal cycle lasts 21-35 days; any cycle that lasts less than 21 days or more than 35 days is considered abnormal.\n\n3) EXCESSIVE BLEEDING - Also known as "Menorrhagia" in the medical world. It is a condition in which period flow lasts for more than 7 days or heavy bleeding necessitates frequent pad/tampon changes throughout the day, which can be caused by uterine polyp, fibroid, adenomyosis, endometriosis, and other conditions.\n\n4) FERTILITY CONCERNS - One in every seven couples may have difficulty conceiving. Common causes include irregular ovulation (PCOS), poor quality sperm, a blocked or damaged fallopian tube, and so on. Age, overweight/underweight, alcohol/smoking abuse, stress, and other risk factors all have an impact on fertility.\n\n5) STDs - All sexually active women should be tested for STDs on a regular basis. This allows for the early detection and immediate prevention of a variety of health conditions.\n\n6) MENOPAUSAL SYMPTOMS - These include mood swings, body aches, constipation, hot flushes, night sweats, and other symptoms. Although most women do not require treatment for the symptoms, they may be uncomfortable for some.\n\n7) BREAST PROBLEMS - Breast problems such as lumps, pain or tenderness in the breast, nipple discharge or inversion, and changes in the skin of the breast are common in women of all ages. While it can be frightening, breast cancer is not the cause of the majority of problems.',
