    topics: ["Pelvic floor", "Reels"],
    title: "Women's Health Duo ,  Instagram Reel",
    summary: "Patient education from @womenshealthduo on Instagram.",
    instagramCaption:
      "\u{1F538}When it comes to strengthening the pelvic floor, it's important to understand that relying solely on kegel exercises is not enough. \n\n\u{1F538}Therefore, a comprehensive approach to pelvic floor strengthening includes a variety of exercises and techniques which involves engaging in whole-body exercises.\n\n\u27A1\uFE0F A study showed that the following exercises recruited more pelvic floor muscle during it's contraction than by just doing a kegel!\n\n\u{1F538}Here is a list of symptoms which can be associated with pelvic floor dysfunction and where pelvic floor exercises can help : \n\n\u{1F539}Urinary incontinence (leakage of urine)\n\u{1F539}Urinary urgency and frequency\n\u{1F539}Difficulty emptying the bladder or bowel\n\u{1F539}Pelvic pain\n\u{1F539}Painful intercourse\n\u{1F539}Pelvic organ prolapse\n\u{1F539}Lower back pain\n\u{1F539}Perineal discomfort\n\u{1F539}Pelvic pressure or heaviness\n\u{1F539}Muscle spasms or tightness\n\nIf you have been facing any such symptoms and want an effective treatment then consult your Gynecologist and a Women's Health Physiotherapist who can help you assess, diagnose and treat you with correct intervention.\n\n\u{1F48C}DM us to know more!\u2764\uFE0F",
